<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<odoo>
    <data noupdate="1">

        <!--
            The position read at a stop had no record at all in the processing
            register, although it is the only piece of personal information in
            the module that bears on the WORKER rather than on the customer.
            Measured on a real base: dozens of purposes, dozens of
            calendar rules, none of them about it. This module aside, 16 other
            applications carry a privacy bridge, and this one carried none.

            ⚠️ No consent to collect. Section 8.1 of Quebec's Law 25 asks that
            the person be informed beforehand of the use of the technology and
            told what means are offered to activate it; it does not ask for
            consent. And an employee's consent to being located by their
            employer is not free: presenting it as consent would suggest that a
            refusal carries no consequence. What is really offered is simpler,
            namely that the browser's permission stays theirs and that a refusal
            lets the mark go through without a position. Same reasoning as
            `sms_call_archive` and `surveillance`.

            ⚠️ company_id empty, on all the records of this file. The watcher
            that erases sweeps EVERY company, archived ones included, and writes
            one register entry per company. A purpose or a rule owned by one
            company would have the four others erased under a rule their own
            register cannot show.
        -->
        <record id="purpose_route_position" model="privacy.purpose">
            <field name="code">route_position</field>
            <field name="name">Position read at the stops of a route</field>
            <field name="plain_language_summary"><![CDATA[
                <p>When you mark a stop on your route (done, customer absent,
                postponed), the application reads your phone's position
                <b>once</b>, at that moment, and keeps it with the stop as proof
                of the visit. It does not follow you between stops, nor before
                the day starts, nor after it ends.</p>
                <p>What it is for: proving the visit to the customer, settling a
                disagreement about a delivery, and measuring the kilometres of
                the day. Who sees it: the people who manage the routes, and you,
                on your own stops.</p>
                <p>Your phone asks for your permission the first time. If you
                refuse, the stops are marked without a position, and your
                refusal is not recorded.</p>
                <p>The position is erased at the end of the retention calendar.
                The stop, its time and your mark remain: they are what proves
                the visit.</p>
            ]]></field>
            <field name="requires_consent" eval="False"/>
            <field name="requires_express_opt_in" eval="False"/>
            <field name="default_validity_days">0</field>
            <field name="context_scope">general</field>
            <field name="channel_scope">all</field>
            <field name="sequence">26</field>
            <field name="company_id" eval="False"/>
        </record>

        <!--
            ⚠️ The term declared here documents the regime. What ERASES is
            bf_route's watcher, and the term it applies is the company's setting
            (bf_route_position_days), not this number. The two must say the same
            thing, and since 18.0.1.1.0 they are held equal by a constraint in
            models/res_company.py rather than by a comment asking someone to
            check: a sealed destruction entry that cites this rule while naming
            another number proves only that the declaration was not kept.

            At most 90 days, for the reasons set out in the module's description. ⚠️ A ceiling, not a
            floor. Section 23 of Law 25 asks for destruction once the purpose is
            accomplished, so a shorter setting is minimisation and stays true;
            only a longer one makes this declaration false, and that is the only
            direction the bridge refuses.

            ⚠️ `data_categories` and `notes` below are in FRENCH, in a module
            written in English source. Neither field is `translate=True` on
            `privacy.retention.policy`, so no catalogue can carry them and the
            module's `.pot` holds no entry for them. The nine other policies of
            this register are in French. Same reason, same decision, as ROU-001
            in the other data file.

            `trigger_on` only accepts expiration / withdrawal / both. It is
            "expiration": the term runs from the marking of the stop, not from a
            consent that is never asked for.
        -->
        <record id="retention_policy_route_position" model="privacy.retention.policy">
            <field name="purpose_id" ref="purpose_route_position"/>
            <field name="retention_days">90</field>
            <field name="trigger_on">expiration</field>
            <field name="destruction_method">delete</field>
            <field name="data_categories">Latitude, longitude et précision en mètres relevées au téléphone de la personne sur la route, au moment où elle marque un arrêt. Rattachées à l'arrêt, donc au client visité, à la date, à l'heure et à la personne qui a marqué.</field>
            <field name="notes">Au plus 90 jours, la durée de ROU-001. ⚠️ Ce nombre documente le plafond ; l'effacement est fait par le cron de `bf_route` selon le réglage « Garder les positions » de la société. Une société qui relève des positions ne peut pas être réglée plus longtemps que ce qui est déclaré ici, et ne peut pas commencer à relever si rien n'est déclaré ; une durée plus courte est acceptée telle quelle, c'est de la minimisation, et l'entrée de destruction nomme la durée appliquée. ⚠️ L'effacement n'atteint ni la copie qu'une marque en attente de réseau garde dans le navigateur du téléphone, ni les sauvegardes, le temps de leur rotation.</field>
            <field name="company_id" eval="False"/>
        </record>

        <!--
            The second purpose is a SECONDARY USE, and that is why it is declared
            on its own: the position was collected to prove a visit, and it is
            used to place the customer visited.

            Section 12 para. 2 (1) of Law 25: information may be used for a
            purpose other than the one it was collected for when that purpose is
            compatible, the test being a direct and relevant connection with the
            original purpose. Proving one went to a customer and knowing where
            that customer is have that connection. On a real base, a small
            minority of contacts were geocoded, the rest having defeated the
            geocoder.

            ⚠️ The proposal never writes itself: a person accepts it, into the
            street fallback field, and never from an imprecise reading. Without
            that reserve, a reading taken 400 m away would move a customer's
            record.

            ⚠️ And the copy that lands on the customer OUTLIVES the position it
            came from, because from that moment it is the customer's address.
            The destruction entry says so, the notice the person reads says so,
            and the contact carries the date it happened.
        -->
        <record id="purpose_route_place_from_position" model="privacy.purpose">
            <field name="code">route_place_from_position</field>
            <field name="name">Customer's place proposed from a position read</field>
            <field name="plain_language_summary"><![CDATA[
                <p>When a customer's record has no coordinates and their address
                could not be found automatically, the position read at their
                stop can be proposed as their place on the map.</p>
                <p>It is a proposal: someone on the team accepts or refuses it,
                and nothing is written without that acceptance. A reading that
                is too imprecise is not proposed at all.</p>
                <p>What is kept in the end is the customer's place, not the
                position of the person who marked the stop. But that copy stays
                with the customer: it has become their address, and it is not
                erased with the position it came from. The customer's record
                carries the date it was accepted.</p>
            ]]></field>
            <field name="requires_consent" eval="False"/>
            <field name="requires_express_opt_in" eval="False"/>
            <field name="default_validity_days">0</field>
            <field name="context_scope">general</field>
            <field name="channel_scope">all</field>
            <field name="sequence">27</field>
            <field name="company_id" eval="False"/>
        </record>

        <!--
            The third purpose, and the one this register was missing until
            18.0.1.1.0: the kilometres of the day are measured from the
            positions read, from one mark to the next, and that figure is
            printed in the kilometre register. It is a second secondary use
            under s. 12 para. 2 (1), with the same direct connection: the
            readings prove the visits, and the road between those visits is the
            road that was driven.

⚠️ An earlier writing of this module declared no rule for the figure and
            said it "follows the company's kilometre register". No rule of that
            calendar covered vehicles or route days,
            so the sentence pointed at nothing, which is exactly the defect this
            module refuses elsewhere with another hat on. ROU-002 now declares
            the regime, six years, the term of books and records under Quebec
            and federal tax law. 🔴 Nothing applies it yet: no cron destroys a
            route day. The rule says so in its own notes, and makes the gap
            visible instead of silent.

            ⚠️ A distance is not a place. The figure says how far the day drove,
            not where it went, which is why it survives the erasure of the
            readings it was computed from and why the destruction entry names it
            among the survivors instead of pretending everything left.
        -->
        <record id="purpose_route_road_measured" model="privacy.purpose">
            <field name="code">route_road_measured</field>
            <field name="name">Kilometres of a day measured from the positions read</field>
            <field name="plain_language_summary"><![CDATA[
                <p>The road between the positions read at your stops is
                measured, in the order you marked them, and kept on the day as
                a number of kilometres, beside the odometer readings.</p>
                <p>What it is for: comparing the kilometres claimed with the
                road actually driven, in the register the company keeps for its
                vehicles. It is never added into the odometer's total.</p>
                <p>This number is a distance, not a place: it says how far the
                day drove, not where it went. It stays on the day after the
                positions themselves have been erased, and it is kept six years
                under rule ROU-002, the term the tax law sets for books and
                records.</p>
            ]]></field>
            <field name="requires_consent" eval="False"/>
            <field name="requires_express_opt_in" eval="False"/>
            <field name="default_validity_days">0</field>
            <field name="context_scope">general</field>
            <field name="channel_scope">all</field>
            <field name="sequence">28</field>
            <field name="company_id" eval="False"/>
        </record>

        <!--
            La politique de conservation des kilomètres mesurés : six ans, soit
            2 190 jours, la durée de ROU-002. ⚠️ `data_categories` et `notes` ne
            sont pas traduisibles sur ce modèle, d'où le français, comme pour
            la politique de ROU-001 ci-dessus.
        -->
        <record id="retention_policy_route_road_measured" model="privacy.retention.policy">
            <field name="purpose_id" ref="purpose_route_road_measured"/>
            <field name="retention_days">2190</field>
            <field name="trigger_on">expiration</field>
            <field name="destruction_method">delete</field>
            <field name="data_categories">Nombre de kilomètres d'une journée de route, mesuré entre les positions relevées aux arrêts et les points de dépôt. Rattaché à la journée, donc à la date et à la personne qui l'a faite. Une distance, pas un lieu.</field>
            <field name="notes">Six ans, soit 2 190 jours, la durée de ROU-002 et celle des livres et registres exigée par la loi fiscale. 🔴 Aucune automatisation ne l'applique : aucun cron ne détruit de journée de route. La politique déclare le régime et rend le trou visible ; l'effacement se fait à la main ou dans un lot ultérieur. ⚠️ Le chiffre survit volontairement à l'effacement des positions dont il est dérivé (ROU-001, au plus 90 jours) parce qu'il ne permet pas de situer la personne.</field>
            <field name="company_id" eval="False"/>
        </record>

    </data>
</odoo>
